WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.917
It is a cold and bleak day here.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.920
There are lots of towns and villages
around the world that once existed,

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.290
but have been lost to the sea
or under floodwaters.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.219
But here, on the south-east coast of Britain,

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:20.199
there's a village that's still around
but won't be for too long.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:23.160
Up behind those cliffs is Covehithe.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.500
It's a picturesque little village.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.939
There's the ruins of a church
more than 600 years old,

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:29.079
with a more recent church built inside.

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:32.710
And by more recent, I mean the little church
is only 400 years old.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.879
There's farmland, a few houses,
a couple of dozen residents.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:39.010
This has been a settlement for least
a thousand years, possibly more.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.850
And it'll be gone by the end of the century.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.650
You can see on old maps just how much land
has already been lost to the sea.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.660
The cliffs here get eroded a little bit
with every high tide,

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.570
steadily being pushed
back and back and back.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.170
There are warning signs to stop
people from walking on top,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.399
because the ground near the edge
will be loose and could fall

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:59.070
with just a little bit
of pressure from above.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.170
And this has been happening
for centuries.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.609
It can't go on forever:
at some point, no-one knows when,

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:06.020
the process should stabilise
at a new equilibrium.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.030
But no-one can model that far ahead.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.970
For the forseeable future, the
local council's management plan

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:16.310
estimates that the coastline will move inland
by about four or five metres every year.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.120
There's a lot of margin for error,
but even in the best-case scenario,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.640
Covehithe is gone in 100 years.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.870
Worst-case, 30 years.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:26.310
It won't actually crumble into the ocean:
to avoid pollution, buildings around here

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.270
are demolished and taken away
when their time comes.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.390
And presumably someone will have to move the
church's graveyard.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.960
So why doesn't anyone save Covehithe?

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.680
The landowners here did offer to build
their own coastal defences back in 2011,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.500
to add breakwaters here as an experiment
to see if the erosion could be slowed,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.590
but they were turned down by the council.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.870
This beach, where they'd need to build,
is owned by the government.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.900
And the government said no because...

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.520
the needs of the many
outweigh the needs of the few.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.000
A little way down the coast is Southwold,
home to a thousand people and a lot of tourism.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.150
And the simulations say that
if Covehithe stops eroding,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.000
then Southwold would probably start.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.840
Or at least, they'd need much, much
stronger defences.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.520
It's not practical to save
every building everywhere:

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.709
this is miles of coastline and
they'd need to defend all of it,

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:11.620
constantly, into eternity.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.829
The council's plan is clear:

00:02:12.829 --> 00:02:16.590
"it is not considered sustainable
to attempt to manage the erosion".

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.239
I did ask both the landowners
and the council for a comment,

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:20.349
but neither got back to me.

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:23.530
Perhaps because Covehithe
isn't a tourist attraction,

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.390
and I don't think they'd want to be.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.239
There are no amenities for visitors,
there's no little tea shop by the beach.

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:32.890
But if you do happen to be somewhere
in the area in the next few years,

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.430
it might be worth stopping by
to see Covehithe... while you can.

